## **Phase 1: Define the Foundation (Requirements & Architecture)**

### Step 1: Understand the business and technical requirements [us.nttdata](https://us.nttdata.com/en/blog/2021/june/design-principles-for-azure-landing-zones)

Before writing any code or creating any resources, gather requirements:

**Questions to answer:**
- How many teams will use this platform?
- What applications will run (microservices, legacy apps, databases)?
- What are the compliance requirements? (finance, healthcare, PCI-DSS, etc.)
- What are the availability targets? (99.9%, 99.95%, 99.99%)
- What regions are needed? (for latency, data residency, disaster recovery)
- What is the connectivity model? (internet-facing, private only, hybrid cloud)
- What are the cost constraints?

**Output:**
- A one-page requirements doc covering workload types, compliance needs, availability targets, and regions.

***

### Step 2: Choose the network topology [learn.microsoft](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/networking/architecture/hub-spoke)

**Decision: Hub-Spoke vs. Virtual WAN**

For most enterprises, **hub-spoke** is the right choice because:
- It's simpler and cheaper than Virtual WAN.
- It gives you full control over routing and security.
- It scales well for 5–20 spokes (application landing zones).

**Hub-Spoke Design:**
- **Hub VNet**: Centralized network containing shared services (Azure Firewall, VPN Gateway, Bastion, DNS forwarders, monitoring).
- **Spoke VNets**: One per team or application workload, isolated from each other.
- **VNet Peering**: Hub ↔ Spoke connectivity with gateway transit enabled. [azuresecurityarchitect](https://azuresecurityarchitect.com/azure-landing-zone/building-an-azure-landing-zone-in-an-existing-hub-and-spoke-architecture/)

**IP Address Planning:**
- Reserve a large CIDR block (e.g., `10.0.0.0/8`).
- Allocate subnets logically:
  - Hub: `10.0.0.0/16`
  - Spoke 1: `10.1.0.0/16`
  - Spoke 2: `10.2.0.0/16`
  - OpenShift Cluster 1: `10.10.0.0/16`
  - OpenShift Cluster 2: `10.20.0.0/16`

**Output:**
- Network diagram showing hub, spokes, and peering.
- IP address allocation table.

***

### Step 3: Design the subscription and management group structure [azuresecurityarchitect](https://azuresecurityarchitect.com/azure-landing-zone/building-an-azure-landing-zone-in-an-existing-hub-and-spoke-architecture/)

**Management Group Hierarchy:**
```
Root Management Group
├── Platform (shared services)
│   ├── Connectivity (hub networking, firewall)
│   ├── Identity (Active Directory, DNS)
│   └── Management (monitoring, backup)
├── Landing Zones (workloads)
│   ├── Corp (internal apps)
│   ├── Online (internet-facing apps)
│   └── OpenShift (container platform)
└── Sandbox (dev/test, less strict policies)
```

**Why this matters:**
- Azure Policies applied at management group level cascade down.
- Billing and cost tracking are easier per subscription.
- Each subscription can have different RBAC and governance. [azuresecurityarchitect](https://azuresecurityarchitect.com/azure-landing-zone/building-an-azure-landing-zone-in-an-existing-hub-and-spoke-architecture/)

**Output:**
- Management group structure diagram.
- Subscription naming convention (e.g., `sub-corp-prod-001`, `sub-openshift-aro-eastus`).

