## **Phase 2: Build the Hub Network (Centralized Shared Services)**

### Step 4: Provision the hub VNet and subnets [azuresecurityarchitect](https://azuresecurityarchitect.com/azure-landing-zone/building-an-azure-landing-zone-in-an-existing-hub-and-spoke-architecture/)

**Using Terraform (modular approach):**

Create a Terraform module for the hub network:

```hcl
# modules/hub-network/main.tf
resource "azurerm_virtual_network" "hub" {
  name                = var.hub_vnet_name
  location            = var.location
  resource_group_name = var.resource_group_name
  address_space       = ["10.0.0.0/16"]
}

resource "azurerm_subnet" "firewall" {
  name                 = "AzureFirewallSubnet"  # Must be this exact name
  resource_group_name  = var.resource_group_name
  virtual_network_name = azurerm_virtual_network.hub.name
  address_prefixes     = ["10.0.1.0/26"]
}

resource "azurerm_subnet" "gateway" {
  name                 = "GatewaySubnet"  # Must be this exact name
  resource_group_name  = var.resource_group_name
  virtual_network_name = azurerm_virtual_network.hub.name
  address_prefixes     = ["10.0.2.0/27"]
}

resource "azurerm_subnet" "bastion" {
  name                 = "AzureBastionSubnet"
  resource_group_name  = var.resource_group_name
  virtual_network_name = azurerm_virtual_network.hub.name
  address_prefixes     = ["10.0.3.0/27"]
}
```

**Why:**
- Azure Firewall and Gateway subnets have mandatory naming requirements.
- Bastion provides secure RDP/SSH access without public IPs on VMs. [learn.microsoft](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/networking/architecture/hub-spoke)

***

### Step 5: Deploy Azure Firewall for centralized egress control [learn.microsoft](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/networking/architecture/hub-spoke)

```hcl
# modules/hub-network/firewall.tf
resource "azurerm_firewall" "hub" {
  name                = var.firewall_name
  location            = var.location
  resource_group_name = var.resource_group_name
  sku_name            = "AZFW_VNet"
  sku_tier            = "Standard"

  ip_configuration {
    name                 = "firewall-ipconfig"
    subnet_id            = azurerm_subnet.firewall.id
    public_ip_address_id = azurerm_public_ip.firewall.id
  }
}

resource "azurerm_firewall_policy" "hub" {
  name                = "${var.firewall_name}-policy"
  resource_group_name = var.resource_group_name
  location            = var.location
}
```

**Why:**
- All outbound internet traffic from spokes routes through the hub firewall.
- Centralized logging and threat detection.
- Prevents data exfiltration. [learn.microsoft](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cloud-adoption-framework/ready/azure-best-practices/plan-for-landing-zone-network-segmentation)

***

### Step 6: Configure User-Defined Routes (UDRs) to force traffic through firewall [learn.microsoft](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cloud-adoption-framework/ready/azure-best-practices/plan-for-landing-zone-network-segmentation)

```hcl
resource "azurerm_route_table" "spoke" {
  name                = "rt-spoke-to-hub"
  location            = var.location
  resource_group_name = var.resource_group_name

  route {
    name                   = "default-route"
    address_prefix         = "0.0.0.0/0"
    next_hop_type          = "VirtualAppliance"
    next_hop_in_ip_address = azurerm_firewall.hub.ip_configuration[0].private_ip_address
  }
}
```

**Why:**
- Forces all internet-bound traffic from spoke VNets to go through Azure Firewall for inspection. [learn.microsoft](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cloud-adoption-framework/ready/azure-best-practices/plan-for-landing-zone-network-segmentation)

