## **Phase 3: Build Spoke Networks (Application Landing Zones)**

### Step 7: Create spoke VNets with VNet peering to hub [azuresecurityarchitect](https://azuresecurityarchitect.com/azure-landing-zone/building-an-azure-landing-zone-in-an-existing-hub-and-spoke-architecture/)

```hcl
# modules/spoke-network/main.tf
resource "azurerm_virtual_network" "spoke" {
  name                = var.spoke_vnet_name
  location            = var.location
  resource_group_name = var.resource_group_name
  address_space       = [var.address_space]
}

resource "azurerm_virtual_network_peering" "spoke_to_hub" {
  name                      = "peer-spoke-to-hub"
  resource_group_name       = var.resource_group_name
  virtual_network_name      = azurerm_virtual_network.spoke.name
  remote_virtual_network_id = var.hub_vnet_id
  allow_forwarded_traffic   = true
  allow_gateway_transit     = false
  use_remote_gateways       = true  # Use hub's VPN gateway
}

resource "azurerm_virtual_network_peering" "hub_to_spoke" {
  name                      = "peer-hub-to-spoke"
  resource_group_name       = var.hub_resource_group_name
  virtual_network_name      = var.hub_vnet_name
  remote_virtual_network_id = azurerm_virtual_network.spoke.id
  allow_forwarded_traffic   = true
  allow_gateway_transit     = true
}
```

**Why:**
- Spoke VNets are isolated from each other by default (zero trust).
- All inter-spoke traffic can optionally route through Azure Firewall for inspection. [learn.microsoft](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cloud-adoption-framework/ready/azure-best-practices/plan-for-landing-zone-network-segmentation)

***

### Step 8: Apply Network Security Groups (NSGs) for subnet-level micro-segmentation [learn.microsoft](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cloud-adoption-framework/ready/azure-best-practices/plan-for-landing-zone-network-segmentation)

```hcl
resource "azurerm_network_security_group" "app_subnet" {
  name                = "nsg-app-subnet"
  location            = var.location
  resource_group_name = var.resource_group_name

  security_rule {
    name                       = "deny-internet-inbound"
    priority                   = 100
    direction                  = "Inbound"
    access                     = "Deny"
    protocol                   = "*"
    source_port_range          = "*"
    destination_port_range     = "*"
    source_address_prefix      = "Internet"
    destination_address_prefix = "*"
  }

  security_rule {
    name                       = "allow-https-from-app-gateway"
    priority                   = 110
    direction                  = "Inbound"
    access                     = "Allow"
    protocol                   = "Tcp"
    source_port_range          = "*"
    destination_port_range     = "443"
    source_address_prefix      = "10.0.4.0/24"  # App Gateway subnet
    destination_address_prefix = "*"
  }
}

resource "azurerm_subnet_network_security_group_association" "app" {
  subnet_id                 = azurerm_subnet.app.id
  network_security_group_id = azurerm_network_security_group.app_subnet.id
}
```

**Why:**
- NSGs provide defense-in-depth at the subnet level. [learn.microsoft](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cloud-adoption-framework/ready/azure-best-practices/plan-for-landing-zone-network-segmentation)
- Prevents lateral movement between subnets even if a workload is compromised.

