## **Phase 5: Implement Governance and Security**

### Step 12: Deploy custom Azure Policies for finance domain compliance [learn.microsoft](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/governance/policy/overview)

**Example: Deny public endpoints for storage accounts**

```hcl
# modules/azure-policy/deny-public-storage.tf
resource "azurerm_policy_definition" "deny_public_storage" {
  name         = "deny-public-storage-accounts"
  policy_type  = "Custom"
  mode         = "All"
  display_name = "Deny Storage Accounts with Public Access"

  policy_rule = jsonencode({
    if = {
      allOf = [
        {
          field  = "type"
          equals = "Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts"
        },
        {
          field  = "Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts/allowBlobPublicAccess"
          equals = "true"
        }
      ]
    }
    then = {
      effect = "deny"
    }
  })
}

resource "azurerm_policy_assignment" "deny_public_storage" {
  name                 = "assign-deny-public-storage"
  scope                = var.management_group_id
  policy_definition_id = azurerm_policy_definition.deny_public_storage.id
}
```

**Other policies to deploy:**
- Require encryption in transit (HTTPS only).
- Deny resources outside approved regions.
- Require specific tags (cost center, owner, environment).
- Audit or deny public IPs on VMs. [azure.microsoft](https://azure.microsoft.com/en-in/products/azure-policy)

**Why:**
- Preventive controls stop misconfigurations before they happen.
- Audit policies track compliance for regulatory reporting. [learn.microsoft](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/governance/policy/overview)

